[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Pipeline Aggregations and Matrix Operations in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Elasticsearch provides advanced analytical capabilities that go far beyond simple metric groupings. Pipeline aggregations process the output of other aggregations rather than directly from documents, enabling complex calculations like moving averages, cumulative sums, and multi-bucket derivatives. Matrix operations, on the other hand, operate on document fields to extract statistical matrices, such as covariance and principal component analysis, useful for machine learning and data exploration."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Pipeline Aggregations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pipeline aggregations are categorized into two main types: Parent and Sibling. Parent aggregations are executed within the context of the parent aggregation and compute new summaries based on the nested buckets. Sibling aggregations act as a peer to the aggregation they compute results from, calculating metrics across multiple buckets."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parent Pipeline Aggregations: Include operations like derivative, cumulative_sum, moving_fn, and serial_diff."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sibling Pipeline Aggregations: Include operations like max_bucket, min_bucket, avg_bucket, and stats_bucket."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Cumulative Sum and Moving Average"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a sales index where we want to track monthly revenue alongside a cumulative total and a moving average of sales over a 3-month window. The following request demonstrates how to combine date histogram, sum aggregation, and pipeline aggregations."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"POST /sales/_search
{
  "size": 0,
  "aggs": {
    "sales_per_month": {
      "date_histogram": {
        "field": "date",
        "calendar_interval": "month"
      },
      "aggs": {
        "total_sales": {
          "sum": {
            "field": "price"
          }
        },
        "cumulative_sales": {
          "cumulative_sum": {
            "buckets_path": "total_sales"
          }
        },
        "sales_moving_avg": {
          "moving_fn": {
            "buckets_path": "total_sales",
            "window": 3,
            "script": "MovingAvg.from(values)"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Matrix Stats Aggregation"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"While standard aggregations compute statistics on a single field per bucket, the matrix stats aggregation is a family of operations that operates on multiple document fields. It outputs a matrix of statistical metrics including count, mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis, covariance, and correlation."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"POST /metrics_index/_search
{
  "size": 0,
  "aggs": {
    "statistics": {
      "matrix_stats": {
        "fields": ["income", "age", "spending_score"],
        "mode": "avg"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Considerations for Performance"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Memory Footprint: Pipeline aggregations hold intermediate bucket data in memory. Ensure proper heap sizing and avoid excessively large bucket cardinalities."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Missing Data Handling: Use the missing parameter in metrics or gap_policy configuration (such as insert_zeros or skip) to gracefully handle missing intervals in time-series pipelines."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Matrix Limitations: Matrix stats are currently non-groupby aggregations, meaning they compute metrics over the matching documents as a single unified set."}]
